    "translation": "In Japan, from June into July there is a rainy season called \"tsuyu.\" During this period it rains almost every day and the humidity rises. Because it causes many everyday problems — you cannot hang your laundry outside, food spoils easily, and so on — quite a few people say they hate the rainy season.\nHowever, tsuyu is not all bad. The rain in this period is essential for growing rice and vegetables. If there is little rain during tsuyu, water shortages can occur in summer and seriously affect agriculture. In other words, our diet is supported by the rains of tsuyu.\nAlso, the hydrangeas that bloom beautifully in the rain are a pleasure unique to this season. When rainy days continue we feel gloomy, but how about trying to notice the good things that only tsuyu has?",
