    "translation": "We often hear the phrase \"young people's move away from cars.\" According to one survey, only about thirty percent of people in their twenties own their own car, a big drop from ten years ago. The top reason was \"because upkeep costs are high,\" and the second was \"because trains and buses are enough in the city.\" Among the people around me, too, many friends have a driver's license but do not buy a car.\nHowever, it does not seem that they have come to dislike cars. More than half answered \"I want to borrow one only when I need it,\" and in fact, the number of people using car sharing grows year after year. In other words, young people are moving away not from cars themselves but from \"owning\" them.\nFrom now on, companies will probably need to think not only about selling cars but also about rental systems and new services. What is being demanded is a way of doing business that matches the changes of the times.",
